  <title>Rebuildable Airflow: snapshot the running container, redeploy with a rollback</title>
  <link>https://williamlfang.github.io/2026-11-11-airflow-image-snapshot-and-redeploy/</link>
  <pubDate>Wed, 11 Nov 2026 17:04:23 &#43;0800</pubDate>
  <author>william</author>
  <guid>https://williamlfang.github.io/2026-11-11-airflow-image-snapshot-and-redeploy/</guid>
  <description><![CDATA[<div class="featured-image"><img loading="eager" src="https://williamlfang.github.io/2026-11-11-airflow-image-snapshot-and-redeploy/featured-image.png" alt="Rebuildable Airflow: snapshot the running container, redeploy with a rollback" title="Rebuildable Airflow: snapshot the running container, redeploy with a rollback" referrerpolicy="no-referrer"/></div><blockquote>
<p><strong>Update 2026-11-11 17:30:</strong> image tags are now the build time, <code>yyyymmddTHHMMSS</code> (e.g. <code>airflow:20261111T172041</code>), so two builds on the same day no longer collide, and <code>./run.sh</code> with no tag lists what&rsquo;s available. The outputs below are from the first build, which still used a plain date tag, <code>20261111</code>. The downloadable scripts are the current versions.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The <a href="/2026-11-11-airflow-outage-postmortem/">outage postmortem</a> had one recurring theme: every time someone rebuilt the Airflow container by hand, it came back missing something. A mount, the concurrency settings, the plugin, the registry login. The image was whatever someone last typed, and the settings that mattered lived only in a running container.</p>
<p>So the same afternoon I wrote three small scripts. <code>build.airflow.sh</code> turns the running container into an image tagged with its build time (<code>airflow:20261111T172041</code>), pushes it, and saves a tarball. <code>run.sh</code> recreates the container from that tag and checks it&rsquo;s actually healthy, or puts the old one back. <code>restart.sh</code> runs inside the container and keeps the scheduler and webserver up. <strong>From the 170 entries in <code>docker diff</code>, the image keeps 13 files (172K). A redeploy is healthy about 15 s after the new container starts, and a failed one rolls back in 1 s.</strong></p>]]></description>
</item>
